WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.230 --> 00:00:05.030
Thomas Jefferson, one of America's founding
fathers, came up with the first swiveling

00:00:05.030 --> 00:00:06.710
mechanism for office chairs.

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:12.290
Decades later, Charles Darwin added small
wheels called casters to his chair to roll it

00:00:12.290 --> 00:00:15.590
around the study as he worked on the theory
of evolution.

00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:18.830
Today, office chairs still swivel and roll.

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:21.740
The office chair has become a status symbol.

00:00:21.770 --> 00:00:24.560
Executive versions have kingly dimensions.

00:00:25.010 --> 00:00:28.700
They're extremely plush and have serious
lumbar support.

00:00:31.030 --> 00:00:37.150
Using a hydraulically powered machine, a
worker bends 14 gauge steel tubing to form

00:00:37.150 --> 00:00:38.560
the bottom cushion frame.

00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:43.430
He joins the ends and welds steel bars to
the middle.

00:00:44.390 --> 00:00:48.290
The bars will support the mechanism for
adjusting the position of the seat.

00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:57.650
He hooks S-shaped steel springs from the
front to the back of the seat frame.

00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:03.020
These sinuous springs will eventually
provide a flexible web of suspension in the

00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:10.790
seat. He positions the rods laterally and
clips them to the springs for additional

00:01:10.790 --> 00:01:12.320
stability and support.

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:15.440
The office chair seat frame is now complete.

00:01:16.850 --> 00:01:20.120
Next, a worker builds the chairs back frame.

00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:25.520
The frame includes a spine, lumbar support,
armrest, attachments and other parts.

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:29.360
He grinds burrs and other rough spots from
the steel.

00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:32.710
In the upholstery department.

00:01:32.710 --> 00:01:37.630
Another worker smooths a leather hide so
that it sits evenly on the cutting table.

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:43.840
He cuts out the patterns following cardboard
templates as he cuts.

00:01:43.870 --> 00:01:49.300
He works around the natural flaws in the
hide, making calculated decisions to minimize

00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:56.590
waste. By cutting carefully, he'll be able
to get all the leather he needs for one chair

00:01:56.590 --> 00:01:58.060
from this large hide.

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:03.310
That's important because color and grain can
vary from hide to hide.

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:08.560
Using pieces from the same one will give the
chair a consistent look and texture.

00:02:09.310 --> 00:02:13.960
Once all the leather pattern pieces have
been accumulated, another worker cuts foam

00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:15.340
cushioning for backing.

00:02:16.490 --> 00:02:19.190
The density of the foam in the chair varies.

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:23.960
He uses more rigid foam for the chairs,
contoured cushioning, so it will hold its

00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:29.000
shape. Another worker selects softer foam
for the part of the chair that the occupant's

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:30.500
back will rest against.

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:38.500
He cut slits into the softer back padding.

00:02:38.590 --> 00:02:42.490
This creates channels that will ultimately
become a design feature.

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:53.050
Using chalk, he draws lines onto the
corresponding leather that match up with the

00:02:53.050 --> 00:02:54.190
slits in the foam.

00:02:57.070 --> 00:03:01.660
A worker aligns the chalk lines in the
leather with the slits and puckers the

00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:03.730
leather as he sews the backing to it.

00:03:04.090 --> 00:03:06.970
This forms a rib pattern in the upper half
of the chair.

00:03:06.970 --> 00:03:16.570
Upholstery. He stitches the padding flat to
many of the other pattern pieces.

00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:20.170
This particular part is a side panel for the
back of the chair.

00:03:24.790 --> 00:03:26.680
Returning to the upholstery with the
ribbing.

00:03:27.010 --> 00:03:30.280
Now he sews contoured casings to each side.

00:03:35.990 --> 00:03:37.920
To complete the back of the chair.

00:03:37.940 --> 00:03:42.890
Workers at a chipboard panel and encase the
structure with foam, then cover it with a

00:03:42.890 --> 00:03:44.060
leather upholstery.

00:03:45.650 --> 00:03:50.810
Once the chair seat has been upholstered, an
employee installs a plastic cover to enclose

00:03:50.810 --> 00:03:54.270
the back. He screws a sliding plate to the
base.

00:03:55.470 --> 00:03:59.850
This mechanism will work in conjunction with
the plate to slide the seat forward and

00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:02.280
backward, depending on legroom needs.

00:04:03.510 --> 00:04:07.260
It also has features for adjusting the tilt
and height of the seat.

00:04:07.890 --> 00:04:11.280
The hydraulic cylinder for adjusting the
seat height is next.

00:04:11.850 --> 00:04:17.220
The cylinder also doubles as the chair stem
and it swivels to allow the seat to rotate

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:19.020
360 degrees.

00:04:19.590 --> 00:04:21.720
He attaches the aluminum base to the
cylinder.

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:27.200
He snaps the casters into slots in the
aluminum base.

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:34.040
He equips the back of the office chair with
an automobile style headrest.

00:04:34.910 --> 00:04:37.910
The prongs fit into the brackets in the
chair frame.

00:04:38.900 --> 00:04:41.390
Finally, he locks the armrests into the
frame.

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:49.110
This office chair is now ready to make
someone's desk job a lot more comfortable.

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:53.050
Vino brew is just what the name implies.

00:04:53.080 --> 00:04:55.930
Vino meaning wine and brew, referring to
beer.

00:04:56.530 --> 00:05:01.000
This hybrid beverage is part of a fledgling
trend by some adventurous breweries and

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:06.040
wineries to merge their two otherwise
separate worlds of alcohol and create a

00:05:06.040 --> 00:05:12.770
unique drink. This vino brew is made from
port style sweet wine, dark, strong beer

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:17.030
known as stout, and another ingredient
that's brewed coffee.

00:05:17.060 --> 00:05:22.430
This concoction is the collaborative
creation of a winery microbrewery and coffee

00:05:22.430 --> 00:05:27.860
producer. The microbrewery grows some 20
different varieties of hop plants whose

00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:32.210
flowers contain the resins and essential
oils that give beer its bitter and aromatic

00:05:32.210 --> 00:05:36.360
characteristics. To begin the beer making
process.

00:05:37.050 --> 00:05:42.480
The brewmaster fills a tank called a mash
tun with water, then ignites a burner.

00:05:44.980 --> 00:05:51.430
When the water heats to around 150°F, he
pours in a blend of different types of malts.

00:05:51.460 --> 00:05:56.800
Malts are cereal grains, such as barley or
corn, which have been soaked to kickstart

00:05:56.800 --> 00:06:04.090
germination. Then, once sprouted dried with
hot air to halt germination, stirring the

00:06:04.090 --> 00:06:09.040
malt into the hot water activates enzymes
that convert the starches in the grain into

00:06:09.070 --> 00:06:10.480
fermentable sugars.

00:06:11.170 --> 00:06:17.380
After an hour or so, he transfers just the
liquid called wort to the boil kettle and

00:06:17.380 --> 00:06:19.960
gradually adds different varieties of hops.

00:06:20.230 --> 00:06:24.820
He brings the water to a boil to kill off
bacteria and sterilize it.

00:06:26.950 --> 00:06:31.210
Next, he calls the wart and transfers it to
the fermentation tank.

00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:34.930
Then he adds yeast over the next 10 to 14
days.

00:06:35.530 --> 00:06:41.020
The yeast consumes the fermentable sugars in
the wort, converting them into alcohol and

00:06:41.020 --> 00:06:43.870
producing carbon dioxide gas, which creates
bubbles.

00:06:44.920 --> 00:06:48.160
This process transforms the wort into beer.

00:06:50.380 --> 00:06:51.730
At the coffee company.

00:06:51.760 --> 00:06:56.320
The hot coffee beans must be cooled quickly
after coming out of the roaster so that they

00:06:56.320 --> 00:06:57.580
don't overcook.

00:06:57.760 --> 00:07:02.230
This perforated cooling pan stirs the beans
to help dissipate the heat.

00:07:02.320 --> 00:07:06.760
Then a commercial coffee grinder grinds the
beans into coarse ground coffee.

00:07:09.080 --> 00:07:11.300
To prepare coffee for the vino brew.

00:07:11.330 --> 00:07:16.820
A worker puts grounds into a filter bag,
saturates the bag with cold water, then

00:07:16.820 --> 00:07:21.440
submerges the bag in water, letting the
ground soak for about 16 hours.

00:07:22.820 --> 00:07:25.790
Then he pours the coffee into a large jar.

00:07:28.600 --> 00:07:34.870
At the winery, Winemakers harvest and press
the grapes, add yeast and ferment them, then

00:07:34.900 --> 00:07:41.020
age the port wine for at least two years in
oak barrels to prepare to make vino bru.

00:07:41.050 --> 00:07:45.820
The winemakers test samples from different
barrels for acidity and sweetness to help

00:07:45.820 --> 00:07:48.370
decide which wines to potentially use.

00:07:50.900 --> 00:07:55.580
The winemaker and the coffee maker then
experiment with amounts of those wines and

00:07:55.580 --> 00:07:59.120
different cold brew coffees made from
various types of beans.

00:08:01.160 --> 00:08:04.220
The brewmaster also joins in measuring out
beer.

00:08:09.030 --> 00:08:12.190
This process is as much art as science.

00:08:12.210 --> 00:08:17.460
For each batch of vino brew, the respective
makers play with the selection of ingredients

00:08:17.460 --> 00:08:19.060
and with the proportions.

00:08:19.080 --> 00:08:24.750
Tasting and smelling the samples to evaluate
aroma, sweetness, bitterness and acidity.

00:08:26.200 --> 00:08:30.220
What they try to create is a harmonious
blend of the three elements.

00:08:30.250 --> 00:08:34.810
A new flavor without the taste of any one
element dominating the others.

00:08:37.370 --> 00:08:41.630
Once they pinned down the winning formula,
they replicate it on a large scale for

00:08:41.630 --> 00:08:49.200
production. The winery pumps the selected
wine from storage tanks into barrels for

00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:50.760
transport to the brewery.

00:08:54.030 --> 00:08:58.740
At the brewery, the brewmaster pumps the
required amount of wine from those barrels

00:08:58.740 --> 00:09:00.060
into a blending tank.

00:09:06.210 --> 00:09:10.380
He pours the required amount of cold brewed
coffee into the blending tank.

00:09:14.180 --> 00:09:17.360
Then he pumps the required volume of beer
into the tank.

00:09:18.320 --> 00:09:23.330
Beer makes up the largest proportion of the
recipe, followed by wine, then coffee.

00:09:25.560 --> 00:09:30.510
After blending the ingredients in the tank,
the brewmaster pumps the brew into a beer

00:09:30.540 --> 00:09:35.190
keg. Then he injects carbon dioxide gas to
add more carbonation.

00:09:36.810 --> 00:09:42.090
When you pour a glass of vino, bru the
bubbles rise to the top, producing foam known

00:09:42.090 --> 00:09:48.480
as beer head. But while this might look and
feel like regular beer, one sip tells you

00:09:48.480 --> 00:09:50.970
that vino bru is uniquely different.

